      "file": "items/cex-yangian-one-root-bethe-equation-does-not-guarantee-completeness.md",
      "item_id": "cex-yangian-one-root-bethe-equation-does-not-guarantee-completeness",
      "status": "repair",
      "strategy": "counterexample",
      "proposed_proof": "**Given:** The Hopf counit representation, its direct sum on a two-dimensional space, and the one-root Bethe-vector notation from [[lem-yangian-gl2-one-root-bethe-cancellation]].\n\n[F1] [[prop-yangian-gl-n-rtt-hopf-structure]]: $\\epsilon(t_{ij}(u))=\\delta_{ij}$ defines the counit representation.\n\n[F2] [[lem-yangian-gl2-one-root-bethe-cancellation]]: the one-root condition is $a(v)=d(v)$, and its cancellation theorem assumes the associated Bethe vector is nonzero to conclude it is an eigenvector.\n\n\n\n1.1 Let $\\mathcal H=\\mathbb C e_0\\oplus\\mathbb C e_1$ be the direct sum of two counit representations and set $\\Omega=e_0$. Applying the counit on each summand gives $t_{ij}(u)=\\delta_{ij}I_{\\mathcal H}$, hence $A=D=I$ and $B=C=0$. Thus the pseudovacuum conditions hold with $a(u)=d(u)=1$, and the one-root equation in [F2] holds for every $v$. [given, F1, F2, algebra]\n\n2.1 For every $k\\ge1$ and parameters $v_1,\\ldots,v_k$, the rightmost $B(v_k)$ sends $\\Omega$ to zero, so $B(v_1)\\cdots B(v_k)\\Omega=0$. The empty product is $e_0$. Hence all Bethe vectors from this pseudovacuum span $\\mathbb C e_0$, a proper subspace because $e_1$ is linearly independent from $e_0$. Thus the one-root equation by itself does not guarantee completeness. [given, F1, step 1.1, algebra] ∎"
    },
